                <p class="snippet" >
                    Referred to: Hickey v. Hickey, [1999] 2 S.C.R. 518; L. (A.) v. K. (D.) (2000), 190 D.L.R. (4th) 108, 2000 BCCA 455; <b>Gordon v. Goertz</b>, [1996] 2 S.C.R. 27; Van Mol (Guardian ad Litem of) v. Ashmore (1999), 168 D.L.R. (4th) 637, leave to appeal refused, [2000] 1 S.C.R. vi; Tyabji v. Sandana (1994), 2 R.F.L. (4th) 265; R. v. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;The Court of Appeal discussed, and the respondents relied heavily on, the decision of McLachlin J. (as she then was) in <b>Gordon v. Goertz</b>, [1996] 2 S.C.R. 27. In that case, the Court found that the trial judge had only mentioned one factor to be considered in determining the best interests of the child. 
                </p>
