                <p class="snippet" >
                     2 S.C.R. 235; R. v. G.F., 2021 SCC 20, [2021] 1 S.C.R. 801; Waxman v. Waxman (2004), 186 O.A.C. 201; Onuoha v. Onuoha, 2021 ONSC 2228, 54 R.F.L. (8th) 1; <b>Gordon v. Goertz</b>, [1996] 2 S.C.R. 27; Young v. Young, [1993] 4 S.C.R. 3; Catholic Children’s Aid Society of Metropolitan Toronto v. M. (C.), [1994] 2 S.C.R. 165; A. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;It should never be considered lightly, given “the particular role and emotional bonding the child enjoys” with their primary caregiver (<b>Gordon v. Goertz</b>, [1996] 2 S.C.R. 27, at para. 121, per L’Heureux‐Dubé J.). I accept, as well, that a child’s loss of contact with a primary caregiver who cannot return to the home &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;She provided reasonable and legitimate reasons for not returning to Dubai, reasons entitled to respect (see <b>Gordon v. Goertz</b>, [1996] 2 S.C.R. 27, at para. 48). (3) Conclusion [170] I conclude that the mother established that the children would likely be harmed if returned to Dubai. 
